<div class="stage-overiew">
  <p>Often in agriculture we are measuring or observing traits in the germplasm we are studying. This can include more typical phenotypic traits such as the plant height or days from sowing to flowering, and also more derived traits such as protein content, microbiome species composition, or measurements derived from high throughput phenotyping platforms (e.g. drone, BELT).</p>
  <p>In this section you will focus on first listing the traits you intend to measure under "Key Measurements" and then fully describing how you intend to complete these measurements. In order for your experiment to be reproducible you should be very detailed when describing your methodology.</p>
  <p>It is highly recommended that you look for a trait page on this site to see how others are taking these measurements. Greater consistency across experiments increases the validity of our comparisons allowing us to make more accurate inferences about the wider system.</p>
</div>
